      ":question/id": "f07d7f91-d30a-5dce-8cae-ea7d095128ab",
      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-66667",
      ":question/problem": "If $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ then what is $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi )-1?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=5$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+2\\cdot 5\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ then $\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi )=\\sqrt{2}.$\n\nFinally, then\n\n$$\n5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+10\\pi )-1=5\\sqrt{2}-1.\n$$"
    }
  ],
  ":knowledge-point/title": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosecant Function"
